concerns are being raised that millions  of poor and underprivileged children in  india  are at risk of being forced into child  labor due to the coronavirus pandemic  child welfare organizations in the  country are sounding the alarm  at a time when significant numbers of  jobs have been lost in india's  informal sector during lockdowns the  fear is  children of out-of-work parents could be  trafficked  to fill the gap here's more on a crisis  that could erase  decades of progress made in combating  child labor  down with child trafficking boys and  girls learn through play with child  welfare advocate rockesh singer  child trafficking is a daily reality in  india  children are sometimes kidnapped others  are forced by their parents to work  fifteen-year-old nuja parveen has just  returned from visiting relatives in the  countryside  how to defend themselves for years he  has been working with a charity group to  fight child trafficking  he says the pandemic has made the  situation even worse  children are in great demand as a form  of cheap labor  when adult migrant workers returned to  their villages  demand for cheap labor increased child  labor is used  to compensate since the schools are  closed due to the lockdown  parents have been sending their children  away to work  this child laborer was found during a  raid on a bakery in delhi  15 boys were rescued from restaurant  kitchens and workshops  in recent months the foundation has  freed more than one thousand children  being held in slavery-like conditions  signs of worsening poverty on the  streets of delhi  child beggars existed even before the  pandemic  but now millions of middle class indians  have lost their jobs and laid off their  servants  these children are helping to feed their  families  kailash satyarthi has established a  children's foundation  which has grown into a nationwide  network its mission  raising awareness among indians about  the need for child welfare  the biggest threat is that millions of  children may fall back into  slavery trafficking child labour child  marriages  school would be denied  these children walk along the train  tracks in delhi  they hope to earn money from selling  plastic bottles  according to unicef there are more than  10 million child laborers in india  most younger than nine years old  that's why rakesh singer has invited  mothers to this event he stresses the  importance of sending children to school  he encourages the boys and girls to  demand a good education  the school was not good at all it didn't  even have water  we wrote to the head and complained  about the lack of water  we even spoke to city council members  about it  i've come here to help find a solution  a school play shows what it's like for  many poor children  how they're drugged and kidnapped  but these children are learning where  they can turn to for help  [Applause]  and joining us for more is mr dhananjay  tingle he's executive director  of the child rights ngo bajpun bachao  andolan  mr tingle your organization claims to  have rescued more than a thousand  children  from exploitative conditions in the last  few months isn't this a job the  government should be doing  this is true but the rescue operations  which have been done  we have done it with the support of the  government only  and because you cannot do a rescue and  you cannot stop or chilling from  any any vehicle cannot be stopped  without the permission without the  involvement of the government so we have  been doing it along with the government  uh surely the identification has been  done by us we have identified  more than about 1200 children we have  rescued in last uh since the lockdown  has started and from first april i can  see that  we have rescued around 1200 children uh  and in last one month one and a half  month the children who were being  trafficked back to work from  behar we have rescued more than 300  children  such children with information we got  from our teams in working in that  source districts from where the children  were coming and  previously the chilean we found the  chilean working during the  lockdown also there were the industries  in which they were they were staying  inside they were working  some of the children were stuck up they  were trafficked  the work has stopped trafficking stopped  giving them food and all that they had  no money nothing  so those kind of children also were  rescued who were the basically the  victims of trafficking and they were  facing problems  so it's a work of the government and  government supported us in  uh rescuing it and that is your right  but  government has been with us right  you're talking about uh these children  who've been working in exploitative  conditions  but i'd just like to know for you uh  from you for about  migrant laborers who already are  struggling to make  ends meet i wonder if they really have  any other option  than to put all available hands  including their children  to work  uh that is true the conditions because  obvious the children were  being trafficked from the source  district was because of the poverty  in the in those districts that was the  reason  now when all the migrants have gone back  the conditions have become even worse in  the  villages we have rescued some children  who are working in the  who have been trafficked for bengal  industry  and while talking to one of them they  said it very clearly that they  my father is alex shapola i'm supposed  to be he's  and we have five brothers and sisters  what do we do about it  i was studying in the school but i have  to leave  and when the trafficker came and said  you know when father offered that he  said he would like to go but  jafika said no they want a child who's  with the nimble fingers  and so he has to be he has to come  though he wanted to study  but he has to come so the conditions in  the villages have become really very  very bad  and uh people when they left from  uh during the